### Oracle Ground Truth Studio — manual test recipes
### Open in VS Code with REST Client extension, or paste into Postman.
### Base URL assumes `python3 dev_server.py` is running on :8001.

@base = http://localhost:8001/api/eval

### 1. List registered providers
GET {{base}}/oracle-review/providers
Accept: application/json

### 2. Meta-generate — turn a production prompt into an oracle prompt
POST {{base}}/oracle-review/meta-generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "production_prompt": "You generate Anki flashcards from text. Output JSON: {front, back, tags}. Rule: if input has >20 words, split into 2 cards.",
  "project_name": "anki_demo",
  "domain_hint": "Spaced repetition flashcards",
  "provider": "openai",
  "model": "gpt-4o-mini"
}

### 3. Run a handcrafted oracle prompt against an input — CRUD matrix demo
POST {{base}}/oracle-review/run-with-prompt
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "oracle_prompt": "system: |\n  You are a Java Spring expert acting as oracle.\n  For each entity field, decide CRUD op based on the route.\n  Output JSON: {entity: {field: {op, confidence, why}}}\n\nuser_template: |\n  Route context:\n  {{ input_json }}\n\n  Determine CRUD ops. Output JSON only.\n",
  "input_data": {
    "route": {"endpoint": "/users/{id}", "http_method": "GET"},
    "entities": [
      {
        "short_name": "User",
        "fields": [
          {"name": "id", "annotations": ["@Id", "@GeneratedValue"]},
          {"name": "email"},
          {"name": "name"}
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "provider": "openai",
  "model": "gpt-4o-mini"
}

### 4. Error cases — empty production_prompt
POST {{base}}/oracle-review/meta-generate
Content-Type: application/json

{ "production_prompt": "" }

### 5. Error cases — malformed YAML oracle_prompt
POST {{base}}/oracle-review/run-with-prompt
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "oracle_prompt": "not: valid: yaml: bad",
  "input_data": {"x": 1},
  "provider": "openai"
}

### 6. Existing oracle endpoints (still work)
GET {{base}}/oracle-review/?suite_id=crud_matrix_llm

###
### ── prod-grade-code-analysis recipes ──────────────────────────────────
### Real production prompt used by the CRUD-matrix LLM workflow.
### File is in the prod-grade-code-analysis repo; for the .http snippet
### we embed a compact stand-in. To run with the full real prompt,
### use the smoke script:
###
###   python3 scripts/oracle_studio_smoke.py \
###     --prompt-file "../prod-grade-code-analysis/src/prompts/chat/agents/crud_matrix_column.java_springboot.v1.yml" \
###     --project-name prod-code-analysis \
###     --domain-hint "Java Spring Boot CRUD matrix"
###

### 7. Meta-generate — CRUD matrix oracle (compact stand-in prompt)
POST {{base}}/oracle-review/meta-generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "production_prompt": "You are a Java Spring Boot CRUD-matrix classifier. For one HTTP route with pre-resolved context (call_chain, entities with JPA annotations, method_access read/write lists), output JSON {entity: {db_column: {op, confidence, reason}}}. Rules: 1) HTTP method gives a strong prior (GET→R, POST→C, PUT/PATCH→U, DELETE→D on id only). 2) @Id+@GeneratedValue → NEVER C/U. 3) @Column(insertable=false) → never C. 4) @Column(updatable=false) → never U. 5) @CreatedDate / @LastModifiedDate → DB-managed, skip unless read. 6) Use db_column when present, else field name. 7) Omit fields not touched by call chain.",
  "project_name": "prod-code-analysis",
  "domain_hint": "Java Spring Boot CRUD matrix from Neo4j route context",
  "provider": "openai",
  "model": "gpt-4o-mini"
}

### 8. Run-with-prompt — CRUD matrix on a sample Java Spring route
POST {{base}}/oracle-review/run-with-prompt
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "oracle_prompt": "system: |\n  You are a senior Java Spring Boot and JPA expert acting as an INDEPENDENT\n  ORACLE for evaluation. For each (entity, field) pair visible in the route\n  context, decide the DATABASE-COLUMN-level CRUD operation this HTTP route\n  performs. Reason from your own deep knowledge of JPA / Spring conventions.\n\n  Chain-of-thought (include in why):\n    1. Identify the HTTP method + entry controller method.\n    2. Trace the call_chain to find every entity method invoked.\n    3. For each field: check read / set / ignored.\n    4. Apply JPA annotation semantics.\n    5. Derive op: C / R / U / D, combine when multiple apply.\n    6. Assign confidence: high if clear, medium if inferred, low if ambiguous.\n\n  Output JSON: {entity: {field: {op, confidence, why}}}.\n  Use ONLY entity short names and field names that appear in `entities`.\n\nuser_template: |\n  Route context:\n  {{ input_json }}\n\n  Output JSON only. No commentary.\n",
  "input_data": {
    "route": {
      "endpoint": "/orders",
      "http_method": "POST",
      "use_case": "POST /orders"
    },
    "call_chain": [
      {"class": "OrderController", "method": "createOrder"},
      {"class": "OrderService", "method": "createOrder"},
      {"class": "OrderRepository", "method": "save"}
    ],
    "entities": [
      {
        "short_name": "Order",
        "fields": [
          {"name": "id", "db_column": "id", "annotations": ["@Id", "@GeneratedValue"]},
          {"name": "total", "db_column": "total"},
          {"name": "status", "db_column": "status"},
          {"name": "createdAt", "db_column": "created_at", "annotations": ["@CreatedDate"]}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "method_access": {
      "OrderService.createOrder": {"write": ["status", "total"], "read": []}
    }
  },
  "provider": "openai",
  "model": "gpt-4o-mini"
}
